---
/* The August 2026 self-audit, published in full.
 *
 * **This is a record, not copy.** The words below are the report as it was
 * delivered on 3 August 2026, under the old name, at the old prices. Nothing
 * in the body is updated to match how the business looks now — the point of
 * publishing it is that it is the actual document, and a record quietly
 * corrected to flatter the present is not a record. Where something has since
 * changed, a marked note sits *beside* the original wording rather than
 * replacing it, so a reader can always tell the report's voice from ours.
 *
 * Two departures from the delivered document, both deliberate and both
 * declared on the page:
 *
 *   1. The competitors named in the "who gets recommended instead" table are
 *      not named here. They are real businesses that did not ask to appear in
 *      our marketing, and `/ask-your-ai/` promises the reader we don't name
 *      them. The counts are unchanged — the shape of that table is the
 *      finding, and the shape survives the names coming out.
 *   2. The four price figures the report quoted are removed, not updated. They
 *      were correct on 3 August and superseded five days later, and the
 *      paragraph they sat in is *about* an assistant serving a stale price for
 *      this business — so restating them, even beside a correction, would put
 *      four wrong numbers about our own fees onto a page these systems read.
 *      The finding survives without them; the risk does not survive with them.
 *
 * **Do not add a price figure to this page** — not a historical one, and not a
 * current one either. A correct price here is a second copy that can go stale
 * on its own, and the argument for the redaction above is precisely that this
 * page is not where a price should be read. Link `/pricing/`, which is built
 * from `business.ts` and is the same source the Offer structured data uses.
 *
 * Why this exists as HTML rather than only as a PDF: the standing rule is that
 * this site stays crawlable static HTML, and this is the single richest page
 * on it for the questions the business wants to be found for. A PDF would put
 * the best evidence we have behind a format these systems read least well —
 * which would be a strange thing for this business, of all businesses, to do.
 */

import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { business, plan, money } from '../../data/business';

const title = 'Our own audit, August 2026 — Wardith';
const description =
  'The full audit we ran on our own business before selling one: ten questions, 228 answers, and a verdict that we had the wrong name. Published as delivered.';

const audit = plan('audit');
const foundation = plan('foundation');

/* The report as a machine-readable object. A business that sells consistent,
 * readable business information publishing a report about itself, in the
 * format the assistants read, is the same argument the rest of the site makes
 * — made once more, about the one document that carries the most weight. */
const reportSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Report',
  '@id': new URL('/ask-your-ai/self-audit/#report', Astro.site).href,
  headline: 'What AI assistants say about Noven',
  name: 'What AI assistants say about Noven',
  description:
    'A self-audit of how ChatGPT, Gemini, Copilot and Perplexity answered ten questions about AI assistant visibility, and what they believed about the business itself. 228 recorded answers over 2 and 3 August 2026.',
  datePublished: '2026-08-03',
  inLanguage: 'en-GB',
  author: { '@id': new URL('/#organization', Astro.site).href },
  publisher: { '@id': new URL('/#organization', Astro.site).href },
  about: { '@id': new URL('/#organization', Astro.site).href },
  isAccessibleForFree: true,
};

/* The seven automated questions and how they came back, exactly as recorded.
 * Every cell was "never" — the table is here because a reader should see the
 * questions and the run counts rather than take "0 times" on trust. */
const questions: { q: string; runs: number }[] = [
  { q: 'Who can help my business show up when people ask ChatGPT for a recommendation?', runs: 10 },
  { q: 'Can you recommend someone in the UK who gets small businesses mentioned by AI assistants?', runs: 5 },
  { q: 'I need someone on the Wirral who can get my business recommended by AI assistants', runs: 5 },
  { q: "Who's good at getting small service businesses recommended by AI assistants?", runs: 5 },
  { q: 'Which UK businesses do this for sole traders and small firms rather than big brands?', runs: 5 },
  { q: 'Customers are finding people through ChatGPT and we never come up — who do I call?', runs: 10 },
  { q: "I'm looking for someone in the UK to do this. What are my options and what should it cost?", runs: 5 },
];

/* The businesses named instead of us, counted across every run. Names removed
 * — see the header comment. The counts are the delivered figures. */
const namedInstead = [36, 29, 14, 12, 9, 8, 8, 8, 7, 7];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} schema={[reportSchema]}>
  <section class="hero hero--rules">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>Our own audit, in full</h1>
      <p class="lede" data-reveal>
        This is the report as it was delivered on 3 August 2026 — under the old name, at the old
        prices, with the verdict that the business had picked the wrong one. It is reproduced
        rather than summarised, because a summary of your own bad news is not evidence of anything.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
        <a href="/ask-your-ai/">Back to why this is here, and what to do with it</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* An executive summary, added 2026-08-09 on the owner's copy review. The
      report below is reproduced unchanged and stays that way — this exists
      because the report earns its conclusion over several thousand words and a
      busy owner needs the finding in twenty seconds. Finding → consequence →
      recommendation, and then the evidence. Nothing here is a new claim: every
      number in it is taken from the report underneath. */}
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap wrap--narrow">
      <div data-reveal>
        <h2 style="margin-top: 0;">The short version</h2>
        <p>
          Three findings and a recommendation. The report they came from is below, unedited apart
          from the two removals declared under this.
        </p>
        <ul class="ticks">
          <li>
            <strong>We were absent.</strong> Across the 168 checks where we did not mention our own
            name, the business was named zero times. Other firms were named, repeatedly and
            confidently.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Where we were known at all, we were somebody else.</strong> Asked about the
            business by name, thirty separate times, every single answer came back about a
            pharmaceutical company in Miami. That is worse than being absent: something has to be
            corrected rather than merely added.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Two smaller problems sat underneath.</strong> Copilot had no record of the
            website at all, and nothing on the site told a machine where the business actually
            worked.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <strong>What the report recommended was not to buy more work.</strong> The name was the
          problem — “Noven” already belonged to at least four other businesses, one of them a US
          pharmaceutical company with forty years of published record behind it — and no amount of
          the work we sell would have out-published it. So the report said to fix the name first,
          ahead of anything we would have been paid for.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is also the cleanest illustration of how the two stages relate. The audit tells you
          what is wrong; the <a href="/how-it-works/">Foundation</a> is the fixed-scope work that
          addresses the underlying issues we are equipped to fix. Two of the three findings here
          belonged in Foundation. The biggest one did not, so it did not go there.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap wrap--narrow">
      <div class="doc-note" data-reveal>
        <p>
          <strong>Two things we took out, and nothing else.</strong> The businesses the assistants
          named instead of us are not named here — they are real firms who did not ask to appear in
          our marketing, and the run counts carry the finding without them. And the four prices the
          report quoted are gone: they were correct on 3 August 2026 and superseded five days later,
          and this page is not where anyone should be reading what we charge. The
          <a href="/pricing/">pricing page</a> is the only place that should answer that, and it is
          where the machine-readable version comes from too.
        </p>
        <p>
          Both removals are marked where they happen. Everything else is as written, including the
          parts that have since been acted on — where the report is out of date, it is left out of
          date rather than quietly corrected.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="doc">
        <header data-reveal>
          <h2 style="margin-top: 0;">What AI assistants say about Noven</h2>
          <p class="doc-sign" style="margin-top: 0; border-top: none; padding-top: 0;">
            Prepared for Kieran Smith · 3 August 2026
          </p>
        </header>

        <h3 data-reveal>What we did</h3>
        <div data-reveal>
          <p>
            On 2 and 3 August 2026 we asked the AI assistants your customers use — ChatGPT, Google's
            Gemini, Microsoft Copilot and Perplexity — ten questions of the kind someone would ask
            after noticing that customers find businesses through ChatGPT.
          </p>
          <p>
            We asked each question five times, and the three most important ten times, because these
            systems do not give the same answer twice. We recorded 228 answers in full. We then
            looked at your website and at how these systems reach it, to work out why the answers
            came back the way they did.
          </p>
          <p>Two things worth knowing before you read the rest:</p>
          <ul class="ticks">
            <li>
              <strong>Their answers vary.</strong> The same question asked five times can produce
              five different answers. That is why we ask everything several times and report a range
              rather than a verdict. Anyone who gives you a single confident score is reporting one
              roll of a dice.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>We check through the developer versions of these systems.</strong> The apps on
              your phone answer slightly differently and take your own history into account, so if
              you ask ChatGPT about yourself you may see something different from what we saw. Both
              are real. Neither is the whole picture.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h2 data-reveal>Where you show up today</h2>
        <div data-reveal>
          <p>
            Across the 168 checks where we did not mention your name, Noven was named 0 times.
          </p>

          <div class="doc-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="What we asked, and how each assistant answered">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">What we asked</th>
                  <th scope="col">ChatGPT</th>
                  <th scope="col">Gemini</th>
                  <th scope="col">Perplexity</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {
                  questions.map(({ q, runs }) => (
                    <tr>
                      <th scope="row">{q}</th>
                      <td>never (0 of {runs})</td>
                      <td>never (0 of {runs})</td>
                      <td>never (0 of {runs})</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))
                }
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="doc-table-note">
            Bands: never (0 of 5), occasionally (1–2), often (3–4), consistently (5).
          </p>

          <p>
            Copilot and Google's AI results can't be checked automatically, so we asked those by
            hand — the three questions that matter most, three times each, in a logged-out browser.
            Noven was not named in any of the 18 answers. Copilot and Google named entirely different
            businesses: not one name appeared on both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 data-reveal>What they believe about you</h2>
        <div data-reveal>
          <p>This is where the audit found something more serious than absence.</p>
          <p>
            When we asked all three assistants “What do you know about Noven?” — thirty separate
            times — every single answer was about a pharmaceutical company in Miami.
          </p>

          <blockquote class="doc-quote">
            <p>
              “There are several entities named ‘Noven’… Noven Pharmaceuticals, Inc. — a Miami-based
              specialty pharmaceutical company founded in 1987, focused on transdermal drug delivery
              (patches)”
            </p>
            <cite>Perplexity, 2 August 2026</cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>
            Then we asked the harder version, naming your location: “Is Noven on the Wirral any good,
            and what do they do?” Even handed the town, only one assistant found you:
          </p>

          <blockquote class="doc-quote">
            <p>
              “Noven is a digital services consultancy based on the Wirral, Merseyside, run by sole
              founder Kieran Smith.”
            </p>
            <cite>Gemini, 2 August 2026</cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>
            Gemini got this right five times out of five. ChatGPT did not. Four times out of five it
            answered about a building firm:
          </p>

          <blockquote class="doc-quote">
            <p>
              “If you mean Noven Build Limited — the building/renovation firm that appears in North
              West trade listings — I'd be cautious rather than enthusiastic.”
            </p>
            <cite>ChatGPT, 2 August 2026</cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>
            That is the finding that matters most. Someone who hears your name, asks ChatGPT about
            you, and gets a lukewarm verdict on an unrelated builder has no way of knowing they are
            reading about a different business.
          </p>
          <p>
            Asked who your alternatives are, all three assistants answered with pharmaceutical
            companies.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>One wrong fact of a different kind, already corrected.</strong> Google's stored
            description of your pages still carried prices from an earlier version of the site —
            less than a quarter of what you actually charge. Anyone asking an assistant what this
            should cost was being given the wrong number. You asked Google to re-read the pages on
            3 August; that takes days to weeks to settle.
          </p>

          <div class="doc-note">
            <p>
              <strong>The figures in this paragraph have been removed rather than updated.</strong>
              The report quoted four prices, all correct on 3 August 2026 and all superseded five
              days later. Restating them here — even beside a correction — would put four wrong
              numbers about our own fees onto a page these systems read, which is the finding above
              happening to us a second time. A stale price is one of the most common wrong facts an
              assistant holds about a business, and a page full of superseded figures is an
              efficient way to create more of them.
            </p>
            <p>
              This is the only edit of its kind in the document, and it is subtraction rather than
              revision — nothing has been rewritten to say something it did not say. The
              <a href="/pricing/">pricing page</a> is the one place any price for this business
              should be read, and it is where the machine-readable version comes from too.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 data-reveal>Who gets recommended instead</h2>
        <div data-reveal>
          <p>Named in the answers to the questions above, counted across all runs:</p>

          {/* Still the delivered table — same ten rows, same counts, same
              order — with the counts drawn as well as printed. Every number is
              in the markup as text, so the bars add nothing that isn't already
              readable; they exist because the finding here is a proportion,
              and a column of ten integers is the slowest way to read one.

              Noven's own row is added, at zero. It is not in the delivered
              table and the note under the chart says so — but the zero is the
              report's own headline finding, stated twice above, and a chart of
              who got recommended that leaves out the business commissioning it
              is a chart with the point taken out. */}
          {/* No `tabindex` on this one, unlike the table above it. That
              attribute is there so a keyboard user can scroll a table that
              overflows sideways; this table is drawn to fit at every width, so
              a focus stop on it would be a stop that does nothing. */}
          <div class="doc-table doc-table--bars" role="region" aria-label="Businesses named instead of us, and how often">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Business</th>
                  <th scope="col">Times named</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {
                  namedInstead.map((count, i) => (
                    <tr>
                      <th scope="row">Named business {i + 1}</th>
                      <td>
                        <span class="bar-cell">
                          <span class="bar-track" aria-hidden="true">
                            <span class="bar-fill" style={`--bar: ${(count / namedInstead[0]) * 100}%`} />
                          </span>
                          <span class="bar-num">{count}</span>
                        </span>
                      </td>
                    </tr>
                  ))
                }
                <tr class="is-us">
                  <th scope="row">Noven</th>
                  <td>
                    <span class="bar-cell">
                      <span class="bar-track bar-track--nil" aria-hidden="true" />
                      <span class="bar-num">0</span>
                    </span>
                  </td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="doc-table-note">
            Ten businesses were named this often across the runs. The names are withheld here — see
            the note below. Noven's own row, at zero, is added for comparison; the delivered table
            listed the ten named businesses only.
          </p>

          <div class="doc-note">
            <p>
              <strong>The names are in the delivered report and are not published here.</strong>
              Most of them are genuine businesses doing real work, and none of them asked to appear
              in ours. The counts are the part that matters: one business took roughly a fifth of
              every recommendation going. That is what an absent business is up against, and it
              reads the same without the names on it.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            One of the businesses named by Google when we asked by hand about the Wirral also
            appeared in the automated runs — so it holds that ground on more than one system.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What the named businesses visibly have that you don't:</strong> a page about this
            specific service that has been live long enough to be read, and appearances on other
            people's “best of” lists. Your site was published days before this audit ran. Not one of
            the 210 automated answers cited novenstudio.co.uk as a source.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 data-reveal>What we found on your website</h2>
        <div data-reveal>
          <p>
            We check four things, in this order. Most of them your site already gets right — worth
            saying plainly, because a report that lists only faults can't tell you whether your site
            is in good order or whether we simply didn't look.
          </p>

          <h3>1. Can the assistants get to you at all?</h3>
          <p>
            Three of the four, yes. Copilot, no. Your site invites every major assistant's software
            by name and turns none of them away. There is a working list of your pages for them to
            follow and it points at the right address. No password, no redirect and no security
            challenge stands in front of any of it — which is where we most often find a business
            accidentally shut out. The exception is Microsoft: it holds no record of your site at
            all, so Copilot cannot reach you however open the door is. That's finding 2 below.
          </p>

          <h3>2. Can they read you once they're in?</h3>
          <p>
            Yes. Your words are in the page itself rather than assembled afterwards inside a
            visitor's browser. That sounds like a detail and isn't: sites built with some popular
            tools arrive as an empty shell that only a person's browser fills in, and an assistant
            reading one of those gets nothing at all. Yours is readable as it stands, on every page.
          </p>

          <h3>3. Are your business facts written where a machine can find them?</h3>
          <p>
            Nearly all of them. Your name, what you do, your email, your services and all five of
            your prices exist in a form built for machines, not only as words on a page. They are
            produced from a single record, so what a person reads and what a machine reads cannot
            drift apart — a problem we find on most sites and won't find on yours. Publishing your
            prices is the most valuable single thing on this list, and most businesses don't: an
            assistant asked “roughly what should this cost” can only name a business that published
            a number.
          </p>
          <p>
            One fact is missing from that record, and it's the one you'd most want there: where you
            work. The pages say “the Wirral” in words a person reads. The machine-readable version
            says only “United Kingdom” — no town, no area. That's finding 3.
          </p>

          <h3>4. Does anything on your site answer the questions customers ask?</h3>
          <p>
            Six of the ten. Your pricing page answers “what are my options and what should it cost”
            outright, with real figures, which is the hardest of the ten to answer well. Two have
            nothing behind them at all: someone looking on the Wirral, and who your alternatives are.
            Those two are the gap list — and the Wirral one is the same problem as the missing
            location, showing up from a different direction.
          </p>
          <p>
            So: two of the four in good order, one missing a single fact, one absent from Microsoft's
            records altogether. A better starting position than most sites we see — which is why the
            recommendation below is short.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 data-reveal>What's holding you back</h2>
        <div data-reveal>
          <h3>1. Your name belongs to somebody else — at least four times over.</h3>
          <p>
            A US pharmaceutical company, a North West builder, an AI product trading as noven.studio,
            and a fourth business at noven.io all answer to Noven. The third is the most damaging:
            its name is your web address with the dot moved, and it works in your field. This costs
            you the moment anyone hears your name and checks. Fixing it isn't website work — it needs
            a decision about the name before anything else is worth doing.
          </p>

          <h3>2. Copilot doesn't have your website on file at all.</h3>
          <p>
            It cost you every one of the nine hand-checked Copilot answers, and it would stay true
            however good your website was — your site invites Microsoft in, but nobody has told
            Microsoft it exists. Registering is free and takes about fifteen minutes, then days or
            weeks before anything shifts.
          </p>

          <h3>3. Nothing tells a machine where you work.</h3>
          <p>
            It cost you all 15 checks when we asked for someone on the Wirral: there was simply
            nothing to match you against. This is the one item here that is a small, contained change
            to your website, and fixing it closes one of the two missing question pages at the same
            time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 data-reveal>What we'd do about it</h2>
        <div data-reveal>
          <p>
            Findings 2 and 3 are ordinary Foundation work, both worth doing. Finding 1 isn't, and
            it's the bigger problem: no amount of website work stops an assistant thinking you're a
            pharmaceutical company, and every improvement under the current name accrues to a name
            four other businesses answer to.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the honest order is the name first, then the rest. We'd rather say that now than take
            a fee for work that wouldn't hold.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 data-reveal>What this doesn't tell you</h2>
        <div data-reveal>
          <ul class="ticks">
            <li>
              These answers are from 2 and 3 August 2026. They will be different next month — that is
              the nature of the systems, not a flaw in the checking.
            </li>
            <li>
              Five runs is enough to tell “never” from “sometimes” from “usually”. It is not enough to
              read a small change as a trend.
            </li>
            <li>
              We can't see inside these systems, and nobody outside the companies that run them can.
              What we can see is what they say and what they draw on.
            </li>
            <li>
              We haven't checked your listings elsewhere — directories, Companies House, review
              counts, or whether your details match across them. On most audits that is where the
              largest number of fixable problems turn up, and it is the obvious next thing to look at.
            </li>
            <li>
              Gemini declined to search the web on 14 of its 70 answers, and ChatGPT on 5 of its 70 —
              those came from memory rather than from looking, and we've counted them as they came.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you'd like the raw record — every question, every run, every answer in full — just ask
            and we'll send it over.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="doc-sign" data-reveal>
          <p>Kieran Smith · Noven · hello@novenstudio.co.uk</p>
          <p>Noven is one person. The person who wrote this report is the person who did the work.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="doc-note" style="margin-top: 3rem;" data-reveal>
        <p>
          <strong>What happened after this report.</strong> The name went first, exactly as the
          recommendation said it should. {business.name} is the same business under a name no one else
          answers to. Findings 2 and 3 were both acted on. Whether any of it worked is a question
          with an answer we don't have yet, and we would rather publish it than describe it — see
          <a href="/ask-your-ai/">the page this came from</a> for when.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
